    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "$|{-4+ti}| = 2\\sqrt{13} \\implies \\sqrt{16 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13}$.\nSquaring both sides gives $16 + t^2 = 52$, so $t^2 = 36$ and $t = \\boxed{6}$.\n(The original answer had a minus sign, which is incorrect.) To find the positive value of \\( t \\), we need to solve the equation \\( |{-4 + ti}| = 2\\sqrt{13} \\).\n\nFirst, recall that the magnitude (or absolute value) of a complex number \\( a + bi \\) is given by \\( \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\). In this case, the complex number is \\( -4 + ti \\), so the magnitude is \\( \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} \\), which simplifies to \\( \\sqrt{16 + t^2} \\).\n\nWe are given that this magnitude is equal to \\( 2\\sqrt{13} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{16 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate the square roots, we square both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = (2\\sqrt{13})^2\n\\]\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 4 \\cdot 13\n\\]\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 52\n\\]\n\nNow, isolate \\( t^2 \\) by subtracting 16 from both sides:\n\n\\[\nt^2 = 52 - 16\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt^2 = 36\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides gives:\n\n\\[\nt = \\pm 6\n\\]\n\nSince we are looking for the positive value of \\( t \\), the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
